Z||000000||turbo86se||01-13-2000||12:29 AM||||Hi everyone, tonight when I was backing out of the parking spot in my 88 coupe, I could feel the steering "slip". It seemed to do this once a revolution at low speed. I was turning the wheel sharply to the right. It only does this at low speed when I'm turning the wheel sharply left or right. Any suggestions?  Here's some info on my car in case it may help: 115,000 miles, original shocks and struts (they're shot and I plan to replace soon), never replaced balljoints, yet, near bald tires (new on the way). <BR>Thanks again,||||reg||5
Z||000001||chris6878||01-13-2000||01:07 AM||||mine does the same   85 gt, all stock<BR>sharp turning at very low speeds....      kind of feels like a ****  on the wheel... or a spot on the tire... i dunno<BR>||||reg||9
Z||000002||chris6878||01-13-2000||01:10 AM||||it sensored the word J*E*R*K....   wow...    just trying to clear the stars up...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||Patrick||01-13-2000||01:57 AM||||<BR>My '87 does the same thing. <P>I'm not sure if I really want to know what the problem is. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||13
Z||000004||Phaeton||01-13-2000||03:29 AM||||At full lock turns the front wheels are no longer tracking the same circle, so one of them creeps out to the limit of its tracktion then hops back to the line.  It doesn't do this on all surfaces but is normal.  Unless it does it on minor turns...oh oh.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Patrick||01-13-2000||03:39 AM||||<BR>That's good to know. I was sure that I had four balljoints and who knows what else about to let go. ||||reg||7
Z||000006||Scott2M4||01-13-2000||08:03 AM||||My 87 does that also.  Probably that cheap-a$$ Chevette front suspension ...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||fiero56||01-13-2000||08:46 AM||||I don't think it's your suspension Scott, my '88 does the exact same thing. Kinda' feels like the front tires catch a small patch of ice, slide a little bit, then re-grip. It only happens at slow speeds also, and I didn't ask, for fear of what might need replacing. I don't think replacing anything up front will fix it either. When I bought the Formula, the guy I bought it from was a perfectionist. He said there was a little bit of play in the wheel, so he had the entire fron end, everything replaced. There was about 7,000 miles on the front when I bought the car, and I have since put about 13,000 on.||||reg||1
Z||000008||ChadMan||01-13-2000||09:17 AM||||My '88 does that too, and new lower balljoints and an alignment didn't change it (but it sure made a difference in steering response and handling!).||||reg||1
Z||000009||deceler8||01-13-2000||10:40 AM||||You'll probably think I'm nuts, but pop off the horn button and check to see that the torx screws holding the steering wheel rim to the hub are tight.<P>I just replaced my wheel with an SE wheel and while replacing it, I was shocked to see how much play there was in the old wheel. It's possible that loose screws could account for the feeling you're describing, but even if it isn't, check them anyway. You'll probably find they're loose.||||reg||1
Z||000010||turbo86se||01-13-2000||10:59 AM||||thanks a lot everyone, I had no idea everyone else's Fiero has the same symptom. <P>fiero56, that's the best way to describe it, like hitting a patch of ice, sliding a little bit, then re-gripping. <P>I haven't noticed my 86SE doing this, funny because I pay attention to everything when driving my baby! Guess I'll have to check the steering wheel, that could be it! Thanks again everyone, this Forum's the Best!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000011||WGraffiKs||01-17-2000||04:43 PM||||Turbo,<BR>My 86 GT does the same "ice-patch" shimmy, but only at full lock IN REVERSE. It works fine at slow speeds forward, but I've ground off a small bit from the steering stops for both wheels.  The grip-slip tango was present long before the turning radius mod was performed.  Does yours do it ONLY in reverse?<p>[This message has been edited by WGraffiKs (edited 01-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||Spyder||01-17-2000||06:47 PM||||Actually my blazer does the same thing, It only does it when I turn the wheel too far to the left while backing up and it makes that popping sound.||||reg||1
Z||000013||fiero56||01-17-2000||08:50 PM||||I don't know about reverse. Never cranked the wheel very far in that gear. Guess I'll have to try it. <BR>Sounds like a GM thing. I come from a (uhh ohh) Ford family. My Fiero was the first GM ever, since my dad was a teenager. None of our (theres that word again) Fords have ever done this, and if a Blazer does it too, hmmm.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Harry||01-17-2000||10:58 PM||||I first encountered this problem with a 4X4.<BR>What really happens is at full left or right your tires are no longer in correct alignment! So what your feeling is each tire fighting the other to steer the car........<BR>Go figure!||||reg||6
Z||000015||Patrick||01-17-2000||11:02 PM||||<BR>Let's not draw too many conclusions here... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>My Fiero is the first of the six GM vehicles that I've owned over the last 25 years that's done this.||||reg||4
